import { html } from 'lit';
export function chatReference(href:(path:string)=>string){return html`<section class="api-section" id="api-chat-guide" tabindex="-1"><h3>Messages, drafts and explicit actions</h3>
<p>Compare the <a href=${href('/api-examples/chat-patterns')}>themed chat demo with full source</a> and the <a href=${href('/workflows/chat')}>contextual Chat workflow</a>. These components provide composition and a send request; applications retain their transcript, transport and editing state.</p>
<pre><code>${`<en-chat-message author="Mira">
  <time slot="metadata" datetime="2026-09-15T09:00:00">9:00 AM</time>
  Could we make the cover image quieter?
  <en-button slot="actions">Use suggestion</en-button>
</en-chat-message>
<en-chat-composer>
  <en-textarea slot="editor" label="Message" rows="3"></en-textarea>
  <en-file-upload slot="attachments" multiple>
    <span slot="label">Attachments</span>
  </en-file-upload>
</en-chat-composer>`}</code></pre>
<p><code>en-chat-message</code> renders a labeled article. Use <code>author</code> for the visible fallback, or an <code>author</code> slot for rich content; supply <code>label</code> as the plain accessible equivalent when replacing the author. Slots expose avatar, metadata, default body, attachments, actions and status. <code>outgoing</code> changes surface styling without reordering the transcript. Message content remains authored DOM; the component does not parse Markdown, sanitize HTML, announce additions or manage scrolling. Keep transcript order chronological and render untrusted text as text, as the demo does.</p>
<p>The composer accepts one direct or forwarded <code>en-textarea</code> or native <code>textarea</code> in its <code>editor</code> slot. That editor keeps ownership of its value, label, validation, form association and native editing. The read-only composer <code>value</code> getter reads the current draft; write to the editor to change it. The composer group label does not replace the editor’s accessible label.</p>
<p>Custom editors can opt in with <code>registerChatEditor(editor, adapter)</code>, imported from <code>@en-reve/elements/chat-composer.js</code>. Register the actual slotted host and dispose the registration when its integration disconnects. The adapter supplies current <code>value</code>, <code>focus()</code>, <code>getSnapshot()</code>, optional <code>reportValidity()</code>, and live <code>disabled</code>, <code>readOnly</code> and <code>composing</code> state. Registration does not supply editing behavior, labels or form association. Unregistered custom elements are ignored.</p>
<pre><code>${`import { registerChatEditor } from '@en-reve/elements/chat-composer.js';
const dispose = registerChatEditor(editor, {
  get value() { return editor.plainText; },
  get disabled() { return editor.disabled; },
  get readOnly() { return editor.readOnly; },
  get composing() { return editor.isComposing; },
  focus: options => editor.focus(options),
  reportValidity: () => editor.reportValidity(),
  getSnapshot: () => ({
    value: editor.plainText,
    content: editor.exportDocument(),
  }),
});
// Call dispose() when this integration disconnects.`}</code></pre>
<p>The methods on <code>editor</code> in this example belong to the consuming application. Optional <code>content</code> is an application-defined structured document made of JSON-compatible data. The composer copies and deeply freezes it with <code>value</code> before dispatching; invalid data throws a TypeError and no send event is emitted. Capture synchronously without modifying the draft or committing suggestions. Save this detached snapshot for Retry; later edits must not change the failed message. The optional en-token-editor uses this adapter. Registering an adapter alone does not load the token editor or any providers.</p>
<h4>Optional tokens and trigger extensions</h4>
<p>Try the <a href=${href('/api-examples/composable-chat')}>live reference, tool and color editor</a>. <code>en-token-editor</code> is a separately imported editor with a versioned <code>document</code>, plain-text <code>value</code>, undo/redo and atomic tokens. Setting either document or value is an authoritative reset that clears history. It does not install any token types or triggers by default. Forms and transport remain application-owned.</p>
<p><code>registerToken(type, renderer, options?)</code> supplies visuals for a token type and returns a disposer. Tokens have an occurrence <code>id</code>, <code>type</code>, text fallback, accessible <code>label</code> and JSON-compatible <code>data</code>. Unknown types remain readable. Renderers return DOM nodes; the editor owns the noneditable wrapper. Keep renderer children noninteractive. Set <code>{extension: "colors"}</code> to let the editor supply a named, keyboard-accessible button wrapper that opens that extension to edit the occurrence. Disabled/read-only editors disable these buttons. Swatch-only visuals retain their color in the accessible label and plain-text fallback.</p>
<p><code>registerExtension(extension)</code> registers a named trigger and returns a disposer. Or set the same <code>extension</code> object on <code>en-editor-trigger</code>, associated through <code>for</code> in the same tree scope or the <code>editor</code> property across roots. Its disabled state and removal unregister it. The extension supplies <code>id</code>, <code>trigger</code>, <code>label</code>, and <code>provide({query, signal})</code> for choices, <code>render(session)</code> for custom content in the editor popup, or <code>open(session)</code> for an application-owned picker. Supply one presentation approach per extension. The open hook runs synchronously from the triggering interaction, so it can invoke a native chooser before browser user activation expires. External pickers own their focus, dismissal and event cleanup; the editor does not dismiss them on outside focus/pointer events. Use the session AbortSignal to clean up when the document changes or the extension is removed. Async results are ignored after their query/session changes. Default matching requires a whitespace/start boundary; <code>match</code> can supply custom rules.</p>
<p><code>renderOption(choice)</code> customizes noninteractive suggestion contents (such as a color swatch); the editor retains the option role, accessible name from <code>choice.label</code>, selection and Arrow/Enter behavior. Do not include buttons or other interactive descendants. Renderer-created elements may expose their own <code>part</code> attributes. The demo exposes <code>color-swatch</code> inside tokens and <code>color-option-swatch</code> inside suggestions; their geometry comes from application CSS. Color tokens expose a checkerboard through color-swatch and a separate color-swatch-paint Part for the translucent fill; only the chosen fill color is inline. Checker tiles use the shared color-slider checker tokens. <code>registerToken()</code> can replace the entire token-content renderer, while its <code>part</code> option names the outer wrapper. Optional <code>select(choice, session)</code> handles selection: call <code>session.commit(choice)</code>, cancel, or <code>session.openPicker(open)</code> to hand off synchronously to an external picker while retaining the original replacement range and AbortSignal. Mode switches and document changes cancel pending sessions. The color demo defaults to a composed en-color-picker, with native-first and typeahead-first alternatives, including names, 3/4/6/8-digit hex, recent colors and a native-picker option.</p><p>A choice has id and label, optional description, and either insertion runs or an application action/data. <code>session.commit(choice)</code> applies the current choice; <code>session.cancel()</code> leaves the draft intact. <code>session.getAnchorRect()</code> returns a current viewport rectangle for the edited token, typed trigger glyph, or manually opened caret (with an editor fallback); external pickers can use it to position their anchor without inspecting shadow DOM. The browser still chooses the native picker’s final placement. In native-first mode, # opens the native chooser directly and commits one edit on its change event. It does not insert a chip on cancellation. Browser chooser appearance and cancellation events vary; the demo also ends a pending session when the user resumes page interaction. Native chooser behavior remains a manual browser review item. The same extension can instead supply render(session) for a fully customizable picker. <code>openExtension(id, {tokenId})</code> edits an existing occurrence; <code>session.token</code> contains that token, and retaining its id updates it in place. Without tokenId, <code>openExtension(id)</code> opens the same extension from a toolbar and restores editor focus and selection, so Arrow keys and Enter immediately control the list. The demo inserts tool tokens; applications can instead return action/data choices when a tool should perform an action without inserting content. While a picker is open the composer blocks send so it cannot capture unresolved input. Default suggestions keep editor focus; Arrow keys select and Enter accepts. Enter or Arrow Down enters an interactive custom picker. A nested custom control can expose data-picker-focus with a focus() method; the editor delegates focus to it. Escape cancels. Tab dismisses a suggestion list; inside a custom picker it moves through the picker controls and dismisses when focus leaves.</p>
<h4>Composed color picker</h4>
<p>The default color extension renders <code>en-color-picker</code> directly. Typing a 3/4/6/8-digit hex value or an exact palette name updates its preview. Picker and Chips tabs separate editable color controls from matching palette/recent colors; changing tabs preserves the color draft. Enter or Down Arrow moves from the message into the hex field; Tab continues through native red, green and blue sliders and the application-owned tabs and actions. Apply commits a single undoable chip edit. Cancel or Escape leaves typed text intact, or retains the original chip when editing it. The editor does not infer that a dismissed trigger was accidental.</p>
<p><code>en-color-picker</code> is also usable inline: its sRGB or Display-P3 <code>value</code> changes through one cancelable <code>en-change</code> event. Hex commits on Enter or blur; sliders update while adjusted. Author writes are silent. Palette/recent slots accept application controls. The picker owns no dialog, form submission or Apply/Cancel policy. Labels and invalid feedback are localizable properties. Shared input, range, focus and size tokens provide theme defaults; Parts expose the preview, hex and each channel, and managed <code>component.color-picker.*</code> theme settings map to <code>--en-color-picker-*</code> geometry properties. <code>checkValidity()</code> inspects the hex draft; <code>reportValidity()</code> reveals feedback and focuses an invalid draft before a containing Apply action. HEX/RGB/HSL inputs and optional alpha use gradient-backed en-color-slider controls. Color tokens preserve validated CSS color strings, including Display-P3 and alpha, across both editor backends. The application validates payloads before rendering or committing; unsupported payloads remain readable text. Native chooser mode declines wide-gamut edits instead of silently converting. Set plane on the picker to use an HSV saturation/value plane with equivalent sliders and exact numeric fields. The wide-gamut editor example uses this mode in both backends; a pointer gesture previews locally and commits once on release, while the outer Apply action remains one editor undo step.</p>
<h4>Token styling and editing</h4>
<p><code>::part(token)</code> styles every chip; <code>::part(token-interactive)</code> styles actionable wrappers, and <code>::part(token-content)</code> styles their noninteractive contents. Register <code>{part: "reference-token"}</code> to expose an additional type-specific Part. The part name is a single lowercase identifier. Use Parts for application layout, and managed <code>component.editor-token.*</code> tokens for reusable themes: background, color, border-color, hover-background, pressed-background, radius, padding-inline, padding-block, gap and min-size. These map to <code>--en-editor-token-*</code> CSS properties. Full theme changes reset unpinned overrides; light/dark pairs retain their own paint. Focus uses the shared button focus family; reduced motion, forced colors and hover-capability gating remain active. Interactive minimum target sizes still apply, including touch targets.</p>
<pre><code>${`editor.registerToken('app/reference', renderReference, {
  extension: 'references',
  part: 'reference-token',
  deleteBehavior: 'edit',
});
editor.editToken('reference-1', {query: 'Mi'});
// The token becomes @Mi, with the reference picker open.
// Omit query to restore just the registered trigger.`}</code></pre>
<pre><code>${`en-token-editor::part(token) {
  --en-editor-token-inline-padding: .5em;
}
en-token-editor::part(reference-token) {
  --en-editor-token-radius: .25rem;
}`}</code></pre>
<p>Token text is atomic by default. <code>deleteBehavior: "edit"</code> opts a registered type into restoring its trigger when Backspace is immediately after it, Delete is immediately before it, or either key is used on its focused button. The associated extension must still be installed. A selected range deletes normally; it does not open several pickers. Explicit <code>editToken(id, {query})</code> converts that one occurrence to editable text as a cancelable undoable edit and opens its picker. Escape leaves the restored trigger/query; Undo restores the previous chip. Selecting a new result creates the token supplied by the application. The method returns false for unknown tokens, absent extensions, disabled/read-only/composing state, or a canceled edit. To edit a value while retaining its chip, use <code>openExtension(id, {tokenId})</code> instead. Arbitrary nested contenteditable regions inside a token are not supported: reference identity and color data must remain consistent with the visible label.</p>
<p>Both editors expose <code>value</code> as the document text, readonly <code>draftValue</code> for live native text and readonly <code>composing</code>. Draft previews should use <code>en-input.detail.value</code> or <code>draftValue</code>; token-editor value no longer switches to unaccepted IME text. Tentative en-change reads still expose the proposed document. Editor action data is detached JSON, deeply frozen before dispatch; compare application IDs instead of provider-object identity.</p><p><code>focus(options)</code> returns void and forwards native options. Toolbar waits for its render; token editor requires its textbox to be rendered, and rich editor requires its asynchronous backend to be mounted. Calls before editor readiness are no-ops; rich-editor updateComplete alone does not guarantee backend mounting.</p><p>Committed edits emit one cancelable <code>en-change</code>; its previous/proposed values are structured documents. Native draft input emits <code>en-input</code>, including composing state. IME is allowed to finish before an authoritative reset is applied. Copy/cut includes readable plain text and a versioned <code>application/x-en-editor+json</code> fragment. Pasting between token and rich editors retains registered tokens with fresh occurrence IDs; unknown token types become their text fallback. Rich formatting is deliberately flattened in this editor. External pasted HTML is never executed or interpreted here. Invalid or oversized structured payloads fall back to plain text. Cut/paste uses the same cancelable change and undo history. Register only trusted renderers and validate application token data before using it. Undo/redo retains at most 100 entries. Typed suggestions align with the trigger’s leading edge, keeping their horizontal position while the query grows. Wrapped queries move the popup vertically only when needed to keep the active typing line clear. If the trigger scrolls out of view, a visible caret becomes the anchor; if both are hidden, the popup clamps to the visible editor edge. Manual opens use the caret and token edits use the chip. Nested scrolling, wrapping and viewport changes reposition controls; RTL and viewport clamping remain supported. Real mobile keyboard and assistive-technology editing remain review checkpoints.</p>
<p>Send uses a cancelable, bubbling, composed <code>en-action</code> with <code>detail.action === 'send'</code> and an immutable <code>detail.data.value</code> snapshot. <code>requestSend()</code> validates the editor and returns whether the request was accepted, not whether a message was delivered. A disabled/read-only editor, an active IME composition, or <code>sending</code> blocks a request. Blank drafts require <code>allow-empty</code>, for example for attachment-only messages. The component never clears text or files.</p>
<pre><code>${`composer.addEventListener('en-action', event => {
  if (event.detail.action !== 'send') return;
  if (!canSend) event.preventDefault();
  queueMicrotask(async () => {
    if (event.defaultPrevented || composer.sending) return;
    const sentValue = event.detail.data.value;
    composer.sending = true;
    try {
      await sendMessage(sentValue); // Your application transport.
      if (editor.value === sentValue) editor.value = '';
    } catch {
      // Show a persistent error and preserve the draft for retry.
    } finally {
      composer.sending = false;
    }
  });
});`}</code></pre>
<p>Enter inserts a newline. Control/Command + Enter requests send unless composing, including the legacy IME key-code guard. Applications can cancel the key event before it reaches the composer. <code>focus()</code> forwards to the editor. <code>sending</code> marks the default send button unavailable while retaining its focus and allowing the user to write their next draft. A replacement <code>send</code> slot triggers the same request on click; reflect pending/disabled on your supplied button. Tool and attachment actions remain ordinary application-owned controls.</p>
<p>Snapshot attachment references when accepting a send, retain them on failure, and clear only the corresponding draft/files on success. The demo inserts the outgoing message immediately. Complete send and Fail send settle that same message; failure displays a warning icon, “Not sent” status and a local Retry action using the status/actions slots. Retry uses the original immutable text/file snapshot and never clears the current composer. No timer or network is involved. Images have thumbnails; documents show a file-type tile, filename and size. Selected attachments expose Remove, and every attachment offers a local dialog preview with a download link. Image decode failures retain filename/type fallback tiles. Blob URLs are released when no longer referenced, on reset and on disconnect. Files and previews are not uploaded. Its failure status is a small polite live region. The transcript is an ordinary ordered list, avoiding replay of the full history. Message <code>focus()</code> targets the named article only on explicit request. New messages never steal focus or automatically scroll; the Chat workflow has an explicit Show new message action. Review announcements with actual assistive technology.</p>
<p>SSR includes the authored body, editor, controls and slots in the initial HTML. Optional empty slots add no reserved layout gap. The library SSR adapter marks an authored or forwarded send slot and hides its fallback button before hydration, avoiding duplicate accessible actions. Later slot changes restore or hide that fallback reactively. Hydration retains the slotted editor rather than moving or replacing it. Editing and submission enhancement require the element definitions; a native form fallback remains the consuming application’s choice.</p>
<p>Both components inherit shared surface, spacing, typography, button and focus tokens from each inspired theme. CSS Parts expose the surface and individual regions; <code>--en-chat-background</code>, <code>--en-chat-color</code>, <code>--en-chat-border-color</code>, <code>--en-chat-radius</code>, <code>--en-chat-padding</code> and <code>--en-chat-outgoing-background</code> allow local CSS customization. Theme JSONs currently use the shared tokens for this family. Streaming transport, rich-text editing, recording, persistence and transcript virtualization remain separate work.</p><p>Token and rich editors expose <code>control</code> for the native editing surface, <code>token</code> for atomic wrappers and <code>token-content</code> for their noninteractive contents. With <code>TokenOptions.extension</code>, the wrapper is a <code>token-interactive</code> button even before registration; it is disabled while its extension is unavailable or the editor is disabled/read-only. Both editors fall back to <code>token.text</code> when a renderer is absent or throws; <code>token.label</code> remains the accessible name. An optional type-specific <code>part</code> supplements these semantic roles. Custom DOM renderers apply after hydration; SSR uses escaped text fallback.</p></section>`;}
